 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; the server assigns each committed edit one event revision and publishes it to a self-hosted WebSocket layer, which pushes the change to connected browsers. A browser that was disconnected reconnects and catches up by replaying revisions from the change store. The board operates the WebSocket layer itself; no realtime vendor sits on the delivery path. Push delivery lands updates well inside the 15-second freshness target, and subsecond updates are a bonus, not a requirement.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Self-hosting the socket layer means the board owns connection capacity and reconnect handling. The change store must hold replayable revisions and publish each committed change reliably to the socket layer, and browsers must reconnect and catch up on missed revisions.
 
